WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.403
Can three questions predict a Slit and Robo mutant?

2
00:00:07.243 --> 00:00:09.963
Is Slit there? Can the axon hear it?

3
00:00:10.723 --> 00:00:11.863
When does it start hearing it?

4
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:15.753
Here is a ledger. Each row is a genotype.

5
00:00:16.733 --> 00:00:18.853
Read across before looking at the last column.

6
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:25.753
Wild type first. A commissural axon barely hears Slit before crossing, and hears it

7
00:00:25.753 --> 00:00:28.033
well after. It crosses once.

8
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:31.817
An ipsilateral axon hears Slit from the start.

9
00:00:32.697 --> 00:00:33.697
It never crosses.

10
00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.627
Try a row. A fly mutant for comm.

11
00:00:38.747 --> 00:00:41.387
Slit is there. When does the axon hear it?

12
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:52.170
Too early. It is repelled before crossing, and almost no rungs form.

13
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:56.883
Next row. A fly lacking both robo and comm.

14
00:00:58.163 --> 00:00:58.963
Predict.

15
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:10.017
Like robo. Robo2 is still there, so axons are pushed out, and some come back.

16
00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:14.527
Now a fly making Comm in every neuron, at a modest level.

17
00:01:15.387 --> 00:01:17.207
Can the axon hear Slit after crossing?

18
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.400
Only weakly. Robo is kept low, so axons cross and may come back.

19
00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.360
The ladder looks like robo.

20
00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:33.563
The mouse rows read the same way.

21
00:01:34.203 --> 00:01:37.823
With no Slits, there is no Slit push, so axons stall or recross.

22
00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:42.907
With no Robo3, axons hear too early, and almost none cross.

23
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.020
The last row is where the rule fails.

24
00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.880
Remove Robo1, Robo2 and Robo3 together.

25
00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:56.010
The rule predicts that some crossing returns, because the receptors acting too early

26
00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.010
are gone.

27
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:00.213
In the spinal cord, crossing partly returns.

28
00:02:01.193 --> 00:02:04.093
In hindbrain precerebellar axons, it does not.

29
00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:07.583
So there, Robo3 must do more than mute.

30
00:02:08.463 --> 00:02:13.463
It also strengthens netrin attraction, which removing Robo1 and Robo2 cannot

31
00:02:13.463 --> 00:02:14.463
replace.

32
00:02:14.917 --> 00:02:18.657
That points to the rule's main limit: it covers repulsion only.

33
00:02:19.577 --> 00:02:22.217
After crossing, pull and push must tip toward leaving.

34
00:02:23.137 --> 00:02:25.117
How that happens is still being worked out.

35
00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:27.750
So what makes the crossing final?

36
00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:32.750
The neuron changes a receptor on its own growth cone, and keeps the change.

37
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.190
In flies, the neuron stops making Comm.

38
00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:40.470
In mice, the form of Robo3 that muted the others disappears.

39
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:45.243
The takeaway: an axon pushed too hard too early never crosses.

40
00:02:46.203 --> 00:02:49.543
One pushed too weakly after crossing may stall, or cross again.
